<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#natron">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Natron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Natron is a naturally occurring mixture of sodium salts, used historically in cleaning, preservation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glassmaking">glassmaking</a> and medicine. Deposits in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> made it a trade good. It was among the commodities carried north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire</a> along the Darb al-Arba'in, the forty-day caravan route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/natron.jpg" alt="Natron" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefan Thüngen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emi_Koussi_crater_natron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
